        payload: type-ref #146 10673:180:54-10679:180:60 name="String"
    declaration: enum-decl #155 11003:186:1-11090:191:1 name="@2::Answer" type-parameters=[] doc="What an `App` returns for a request. `Whole` is a whole response, as a `Handler` returns. `Stream` is a response\nwhose body arrives on a channel. `Upgrade` is what `accept_websocket` makes: its\n`Accept` runs a WebSocket on the connection, and its `Reply` (a request that is no handshake) is sent as `Whole`."
      variant: enum-variant #150 11017:187:3-11042:188:1 name="Whole"
        payload: type-ref #149 11023:187:9-11040:187:26 name="HttpWire.Response"
      variant: enum-variant #152 11044:188:3-11067:189:1 name="Stream"
        payload: type-ref #151 11051:188:10-11065:188:24 name="@2::StreamResponse"
      variant: enum-variant #154 11069:189:3-11086:190:1 name="Upgrade"
        payload: type-ref #153 11077:189:11-11084:189:18 name="@2::Upgrade"
    declaration: type-alias-decl #161 11421:195:1-11482:196:1 name="@2::App" doc="The function that `serve` and `serve_tls` call for each request: the request with an empty `body`, the\n`RequestBody` that carries the body as it arrives, and the `Answer` out. A route that wants the body whole calls\n`read_all`; `app` makes an `App` from a `Handler`. A middleware is a function from an `App` to an `App`."
      target: type-ref #160 11432:195:12-11481:195:61 name="Fn"
        arg: type-ref #156 11435:195:15-11451:195:31 name="HttpWire.Request"
        arg: type-ref #157 11453:195:33-11464:195:44 name="@2::RequestBody"
        arg: type-ref #159 11466:195:46-11480:195:60 name="Result"
          arg: type-ref #158 11473:195:53-11479:195:59 name="@2::Answer"
    declaration: function-decl #185 13115:214:1-13302:223:1 name="@2::proxy_from_env" doc="The proxy that the environment names for `url`, to pass as `HTTP.ClientOptions(proxy: ...)`, or `\"\"` when there is\nnone. It is the only call that reads the environment: a client never does, so nothing changes until a program asks.\nIt follows curl. An `http://` URL reads `http_proxy` and an `https://` URL `https_proxy`; the lower-case name wins\nover `HTTP_PROXY` or `HTTPS_PROXY`, even when it is set and empty (which means no proxy). `HTTP_PROXY` is ignored\nwhen `REQUEST_METHOD` is set, as in a CGI program, where a request's `Proxy` header becomes it (httpoxy); on\nWindows, whose names do not differ by case, an `http://` URL then gets no proxy. `ALL_PROXY` is not read. A value\nwith no `://` is read as `http://` (`proxy.example:8080`), and one with another scheme is returned as it is, so the\nclient that gets it says it is not an `http://` origin.\n\n`no_proxy` (or `NO_PROXY`) is a list, separated by commas or spaces, that says where no proxy is used, and the\nresult is then `\"\"`: `*` is every host; `example.com` and `.example.com` are that domain and its subdomains; an\nentry may end in `:port` (`[::1]:8080` for an IPv6 literal); an IP literal matches only itself, and an IPv4 range\nsuch as `10.0.0.0/8` matches its addresses (an IPv6 range is not read). Empty entries and an entry that is not\nvalid are ignored, one trailing dot of a host or an entry does not count, and the case of a host is not\nsignificant. A `url` that is not valid gives `\"\"`: the call that gets it reports it.\n\nAn `https://` URL gets `https_proxy`, which `HTTP` then reaches with a `CONNECT` tunnel."
      param: param #164 13134:214:20-13145:214:31 name="url"
        type: type-ref #163 13139:214:25-13145:214:31 name="String"
      result: type-ref #165 13150:214:36-13156:214:42 name="String"
      body: case-stmt #184 13159:215:3-13298:222:1
        value: call #167 13164:215:8-13178:215:22 name="@2::parse_url" piped=false
          arg: name-ref #166 13174:215:18-13177:215:21 name="url"
        arm: case-arm #179 13183:216:5-13273:220:1
          pattern: variant-pattern #168 13183:216:5-13192:216:14 enum="Result" variant="Ok" bindings=["where"]
          body: if-stmt #174 13202:217:7-13236:218:1 guard
            branch: if-branch #173 13202:217:7-13235:217:40
              condition: call #172 13215:217:20-13235:217:40 name="@2::no_proxy_for?" piped=false
                arg: name-ref #171 13229:217:34-13234:217:39 name="where"
              body: return-stmt #170 13202:217:7-13211:217:16
                value: literal #169 13209:217:14-13211:217:16 value=string:""
          body: return-stmt #178 13243:219:7-13272:219:36 implicit
